#: ../../../guide/useful-mental-models.md:1
msgid "Useful Mental Models"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/useful-mental-models.md:3
msgid ""
"cuTile Rust kernels operate on tiles. The compiler and runtime map those "
"tiles onto CUDA threads and memory."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/useful-mental-models.md:6
msgid "Tile-Based Programming"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/useful-mental-models.md:8
msgid ""
"CUDA C++ kernels usually start from individual threads and explicit thread "
"indices:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/useful-mental-models.md:10
msgid ""
"__global__ void add(float* a, float* b, float* c, int n) {\n"
"    int i = blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x;\n"
"    if (i < n) {\n"
"        c[i] = a[i] + b[i];\n"
"    }\n"
"}\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/useful-mental-models.md:19
msgid "cuTile Rust starts from tile-shaped regions of data:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/useful-mental-models.md:21
msgid ""
"#[cutile::entry()]\n"
"fn add<const S: [i32; 2]>(\n"
"    c: &mut Tensor<f32, S>,\n"
"    a: &Tensor<f32, { [-1, -1] }>,\n"
"    b: &Tensor<f32, { [-1, -1] }>,\n"
") {\n"
"    let tile_a = a.load_like(c);\n"
"    let tile_b = b.load_like(c);\n"
"    c.store(tile_a + tile_b);\n"
"}\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/useful-mental-models.md:34
msgid ""
"The kernel describes what happens to one tile-shaped region. The compiler "
"and runtime map that work onto CUDA execution units."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/useful-mental-models.md:36
msgid "Tile Programs and Tile Blocks"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/useful-mental-models.md:38
msgid ""
"A tile program is the logical unit of concurrent execution. Each tile "
"program runs the entry function once and processes one region of the output. "
"It can query its per-axis grid coordinates and extents:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/useful-mental-models.md:40
msgid ""
"let pid0 = program_id(0);\n"
"let n0 = num_programs(0);\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/useful-mental-models.md:45
msgid ""
"The terms tile program, tile block, and tile thread refer to the same "
"logical unit. The API uses `program_id(axis)` and `num_programs(axis)`; the "
"tuple forms `get_tile_block_id()` and `get_num_tile_blocks()` return all "
"three axes at once."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/useful-mental-models.md:47
msgid ""
"Tile blocks that fit on available Streaming Multiprocessors run at the same "
"time. The full set of tile blocks is concurrent: their relative order is "
"unspecified, and kernels must not depend on one tile block running before "
"another unless an explicit synchronization mechanism exists outside the "
"kernel."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/useful-mental-models.md:49
msgid "Grid Geometry"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/useful-mental-models.md:51
msgid ""
"Partitioning a tensor creates a logical grid. A `[128, 256]` tensor "
"partitioned with `[32, 64]` creates `(4, 4, 1)` tile blocks:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/useful-mental-models.md:53
msgid ""
"Tensor shape:    [128, 256]\n"
"Partition shape: [ 32,  64]\n"
"Grid:            (ceil(128 / 32), ceil(256 / 64), 1)\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/useful-mental-models.md:59
msgid ""
"Tensor dimension 0 maps to grid `x`, dimension 1 maps to grid `y`, and "
"dimension 2 maps to grid `z`. For tensors of lower rank, trailing grid "
"dimensions are 1."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/useful-mental-models.md:61
msgid "Inside a kernel, the program id selects the logical region:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/useful-mental-models.md:63
msgid ""
"let pid_m = program_id(0);\n"
"let part_x = x.partition(shape![BM, BK]);\n"
"let tile_x = part_x.load([pid_m, k_tile]);\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/useful-mental-models.md:69
msgid ""
"For mutable outputs, the selected sub-tensor is passed directly to the tile "
"block."
msgstr ""

