#: ../../../guide/useful-mental-models.md:0
msgid "HBM"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/useful-mental-models.md:0
msgid "Large global memory where tensors live"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/useful-mental-models.md:82
msgid ""
"In cuTile Rust, you load from tensors in HBM and compute on tiles in "
"registers. The Tile IR compiler and runtime decide how to stage data through "
"shared memory, caches, threads, warps, Tensor Cores, and Tensor Memory "
"Accelerator (TMA) instructions when those mechanisms are useful."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/useful-mental-models.md:84
msgid ""
"let tile = x.load_like(z); // HBM -> tile.\n"
"let y = tile * 2.0f32;           // Register-resident computation.\n"
"z.store(y);                      // Tile -> HBM.\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/useful-mental-models.md:90
msgid ""
"You still choose tile shapes and access patterns. The compiler handles the "
"thread-level mapping."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/useful-mental-models.md:92
msgid "Coalescing and Strides"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/useful-mental-models.md:94
msgid ""
"GPUs move memory most efficiently when nearby threads access nearby "
"addresses. Tile loads are designed to produce coalesced access patterns for "
"regular layouts, so a row-major contiguous tile is usually a good starting "
"point."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/useful-mental-models.md:96
msgid ""
"Strided access can reduce effective bandwidth because memory requests become "
"scattered:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/useful-mental-models.md:98
msgid ""
"Good: memory[0], memory[1], memory[2], ...\n"
"Bad:  memory[0], memory[1024], memory[2048], ...\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/useful-mental-models.md:103
msgid ""
"When performance is poor, check whether the algorithm's logical access "
"pattern defeats contiguous tile loads."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/useful-mental-models.md:105
msgid "Concurrency and Parallelism"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/useful-mental-models.md:107
msgid ""
"Parallelism means work is running at the same time on different hardware "
"units. Concurrency means independent work is in progress and may be "
"scheduled in any order. A kernel launch has both: some tile blocks run in "
"parallel on available SMs, and all tile blocks are concurrent from the "
"programmer's perspective."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/useful-mental-models.md:109
msgid ""
"Host-side `DeviceOp` composition uses the same idea. Independent operations "
"can overlap on different streams. Dependent operations must be chained or "
"placed on the same stream to force ordering."
msgstr ""

